  "plan": "Update the C++ solution to: (1) accumulate candidates into an unordered_set<long long> to ensure uniqueness across all t, (2) iterate t up to a safe limit (e.g., 60) while ensuring L = lcm(1..t) ≤ M, (3) for each t, generate all s-values that are products of primes ≤ t (s-smooth) up to N = M/L, (4) insert L*s into the set if ≤ M, (5) once the set size ≥ k, copy to a vector, sort, and print the smallest k elements, (6) if somehow still short, continue until the limit. This guarantees a beautiful set because every produced number is divisible by all primes ≤ t for the t that produced it, and we always print distinct numbers.",
